### 1. The Modern Standard (Flexbox)
Use this for centering a single element or a row of items. It is the industry default.

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* Horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* Vertical */
}
```

### 2. The Shortest Syntax (CSS Grid)
Use this if you want the cleanest code and don't need complex flex behaviors.

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center; /* Handles both horizontal and vertical */
}
```

### 3. The Overlay Method (Absolute Positioning)
Use this **only** if the element needs to float on top of other content (like a modal or tooltip) and you cannot use a layout container.

```css
.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

***

### → Forward Link
**The Vertical Trap:** Vertical centering (`align-items` or `place-items`) fails silently if the parent container collapses to the size of its content.

**Fix:** Ensure the `.parent` has a defined height. For full-screen centering, add:
```css
.parent {
  min-height: 100vh; /* Takes up full viewport height */
}
```
